From: Loic Poulain <loic.poulain@oss.qualcomm.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_qca: set 'bt_en_available' from pwrseq
 controllability

Patches 2/8 and 3/8 of Loic Poulain's "monaco-arduino-monza: Add support
for LGA WiFi/BT module" v7
(<20261005-monza-wireless-v7-0-5a6de7662dcb@oss.qualcomm.com>, reviewed,
not merged), carried as one because they move together. They are the
hci_qca side of pwrseq_is_controllable() (1014).

2/8 Bluetooth: hci_qca: Rename 'power_ctrl_enabled' to 'bt_en_available'
    Manivannan Sadhasivam <manivannan.sadhasivam@oss.qualcomm.com>
    Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20261005-monza-wireless-v7-2-5a6de7662dcb@oss.qualcomm.com
3/8 Bluetooth: hci_qca: Set 'bt_en_available' based on pwrseq power
    controllability
    Loic Poulain <loic.poulain@oss.qualcomm.com>
    Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20261005-monza-wireless-v7-3-5a6de7662dcb@oss.qualcomm.com

    A Bluetooth device only needs the non-persistent setup if the host
    can gate its power, so that it is actually reset on the next
    power-on. When the power is controlled by a power sequencer, whether
    the host can gate it depends on the hardware wiring, e.g. the
    presence of the BT_EN or W_DISABLE2# line.

    Query the generic pwrseq_is_controllable() helper whenever the BT
    power comes from a sequencer, regardless of which provider it is
    (M2, WCN, ...). If the power is not controllable, clear
    'bt_en_available' so that HCI_QUIRK_NON_PERSISTENT_SETUP is not set.

    [Changed for 7.3: pwrseq_enable() where the posting has
    pwrseq_power_on(), which v7.3 renamed. And bt_power is tested before
    it is read: it is only allocated for the WCN and QCA6390 types until
    the series' 4/8 embeds it, so without the test a QCA_ROME or QCA6174
    probe dereferences NULL.]

[retroforge: takes the place of the hci_qca half of the old 1020,
Manivannan Sadhasivam's "Fix the broken BT_EN GPIO detection for Qcom WCN
devices" (<20260422-pwrseq-m2-bt-v1-12-720d02545a64@oss.qualcomm.com>),
which failed the probe on any devm_pwrseq_get() error. Upstream dropped
that patch because a WCN399x without a PMU node then never probes.
Mainline's fall-through to the legacy flow stays; with our device trees
that flow can only defer, as the Bluetooth node's supplies are the PMU's
outputs, which nothing registers as regulators. Nothing changes on our
boards: every sequencer they use reports controllable (BT_EN is wired on
the WCN7850, WCN7860 and QCA6390 ones; the WCN3950 target has no callback),
so the non-persistent setup and the power-off at BT off stay.]
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -2476,7 +2476,7 @@ static int qca_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 	struct hci_dev *hdev;
 	const struct qca_device_data *data;
 	int err;
-	bool power_ctrl_enabled = true;
+	bool bt_en_available = true;
 
 	qcadev = devm_kzalloc(&serdev->dev, sizeof(*qcadev), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!qcadev)
@@ -2574,7 +2574,7 @@ static int qca_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 		    (data->soc_type == QCA_WCN6750 ||
 		     data->soc_type == QCA_WCN6855 ||
 		     data->soc_type == QCA_WCN7850))
-			power_ctrl_enabled = false;
+			bt_en_available = false;
 
 		qcadev->sw_ctrl = devm_gpiod_get_optional(&serdev->dev, "swctrl",
 					       GPIOD_IN);
@@ -2613,7 +2613,7 @@ static int qca_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 		}
 
 		if (!qcadev->bt_en)
-			power_ctrl_enabled = false;
+			bt_en_available = false;
 
 		qcadev->susclk = devm_clk_get_optional_enabled_with_rate(
 					&serdev->dev, NULL, SUSCLK_RATE_32KHZ);
@@ -2622,7 +2622,20 @@ static int qca_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 			return PTR_ERR(qcadev->susclk);
 		}
 	}
-	
+
+	if (qcadev->bt_power && qcadev->bt_power->pwrseq) {
+		bt_en_available = pwrseq_is_controllable(qcadev->bt_power->pwrseq);
+		if (!bt_en_available) {
+			/* The host cannot gate the BT power individually.
+			 * Treat it as always-on and drop the pwrseq handle.
+			 * The descriptor itself is still released by devres,
+			 * so dropping the handle here is not a leak.
+			 */
+			pwrseq_enable(qcadev->bt_power->pwrseq);
+			qcadev->bt_power->pwrseq = NULL;
+		}
+	}
+
 	err = hci_uart_register_device(&qcadev->serdev_hu, &qca_proto);
 	if (err) {
 		BT_ERR("serdev registration failed");
@@ -2631,7 +2644,7 @@ static int qca_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 
 	hdev = qcadev->serdev_hu.hdev;
 
-	if (power_ctrl_enabled) {
+	if (bt_en_available) {
 		hci_set_quirk(hdev, HCI_QUIRK_NON_PERSISTENT_SETUP);
 		hdev->shutdown = qca_hci_shutdown;
 	}
